# MCP
ai_platform_mcp_settings = MCP Server Settings
ai_platform_mcp_settings_sub = Settings
ai_platform_mcp_enabled = MCP Server Active
ai_platform_mcp_require_auth = Require authentication
ai_platform_mcp_require_auth_off = No - allow anonymous access to public tools
ai_platform_mcp_require_auth_on = Yes - every request requires an OAuth login
ai_platform_mcp_require_auth_notice = When enabled, the MCP endpoint already answers with 401 on connect so clients (e.g. Claude Desktop) start the OAuth login. Required for protected tools to become visible at all. Off = anonymous clients only see public tools and never log in.
ai_platform_mcp_description = Server Description
ai_platform_mcp_description_notice = This description is sent as instructions to MCP clients. Describe what this server is for and what data this REDAXO instance manages.
ai_platform_mcp_endpoint = Endpoint URL
ai_platform_mcp_endpoint_notice = This URL can be configured in MCP clients (e.g. Claude Desktop, Cursor) as an HTTP endpoint.
ai_platform_mcp_discovery = OAuth Discovery
ai_platform_mcp_discovery_notice = Standard discovery endpoints for MCP clients. They return the metadata clients need to start the OAuth 2.1 flow after a 401 challenge.
ai_platform_mcp_oauth_phase_title = Authentication & scopes
ai_platform_mcp_oauth_phase_notice = The MCP server supports two access modes: <b>without authentication</b> only tools flagged as <code>public</code> (e.g. <code>redaxo_status</code>) are reachable. <b>With OAuth 2.1 + YCom</b> (PKCE, refresh tokens, Dynamic Client Registration) an authenticated user gains access to protected tools. The scopes they receive are derived from their YCom groups – configurable under <b>Scope Mapping</b>. Manage OAuth clients under <b>OAuth Clients</b> (or let clients self-register via DCR).
